// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row array_repeat_len1_noncopy_operand_double_drop_run (tier 1). `[a; 1]` OVER A NON-COPY VALUE DESTROYS IT TWICE.
// `let arr: [D; 1] = [a; 1];` moves `a` into the single element; Rust runs D::drop once: n = 1, exit 0. TODAY n = 2, exit 1 — `a` is
// dropped at its own scope exit AND as the array's element. Legal Rust at count 1: rustc's tests/ui/repeat-expr/dont-require-copy-on-infer.rs
// is check-pass for a non-Copy operand when the count is 1 (the Copy requirement applies above 1; see array_repeat_len2_noncopy_says_use_of_moved).
// Count 0 reads 1 (right); count 2 is refused (right verdict, wrong sentence).
// NEIGHBOUR of 2026-09-15e-consume's arrmove (SemaChecker::lower_arr_lit marks each element consumed), ROWED WITH REASON 3 (its own cost is
// unpriced): the repeat literal is built by SemaChecker::lower_arr_fill_lit, a different site; arrmove / consumex leave this program at 2
// (measured), and no arm at lower_arr_fill_lit was built or priced. Found by hand program d09 of that round. rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024 on the twin src/compiler/probes/2026-09-15e-consume/rust/array_repeat_len1_noncopy_operand_double_drop_run.rs: compiles, runs, exit 0.
// ROUND 2026-09-15f-consumeland (landing) DECLINED THE ARRAY-LITERAL HALF: DOORS IN SERIES. The first candidate b3b9ae03 (elements marked moved at SemaChecker::lower_arr_lit, TypeVar elements too, and lower_arr_fill_lit for N >= 1) closed this row, but LEAKED six legal programs that are right on base only by cancellation — `[a, b].len()`, `[a, b][1].v`, `for d in [a, b]`, `{ [a, b] }[0].v`, `&[a, b]`, `[a; 1][0].v` read n = 11 -> 0 (1 -> 0), rustc 11 each (pass fixtures bc_0915f_consumeland_hb_{k08,k09,k10,t11,t13,u07}_admit). The door upstream is row aggregate_literal_temp_place_base_never_dropped_run: an array / tuple literal temporary in a place position is never dropped. The lower_arr_fill_lit arm (mark the operand when N >= 1, after the re-lowers) closed this row (2 -> 1) and made `[a; 1][0].v` leak (u07 1 -> 0).
package array_repeat_len1_noncopy_operand_double_drop_run;
struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c + self.v; } } }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    {
        let a: D = D { v: 1i64, c: p };
        let arr: [D; 1] = [a; 1];
        if arr[0].v != 1i64 { return 7i32; }
    }
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    if got != 1i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
